<!-- PROBLEM -->
<section class="section bg-alt">
  <div class="inner">
    <span class="eyebrow">Why readiness fails</span>
    <h2 class="section-h">Mandate readiness stalls for the same four reasons.</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">The mandate is known. The deadline is visible. Nothing moves because nobody owns the translation from regulation to operational action - and every function assumes someone else is covering it.</p>

    <div class="problem-grid">
      <div class="problem-card">
        <div class="p-icon">⇄</div>
        <h3>No single owner</h3>
        <p>Tax interprets. Finance Ops escalates. IT configures. Nobody coordinates the full chain and action sits in the gap between functions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="problem-card">
        <div class="p-icon">⊙</div>
        <h3>Local visibility gap</h3>
        <p>Local accounting partners handle each country. HQ has no structured view of readiness across entities. Problems surface too late to fix cleanly.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="problem-card">
        <div class="p-icon">⊟</div>
        <h3>ERP assumptions that do not hold</h3>
        <p>Many ERPs do not natively support new mandate formats. SIRET mismatches, missing connectors, and format gaps generate exception volume from day one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="problem-card">
        <div class="p-icon">↧</div>
        <h3>HQ deprioritisation</h3>
        <p>HQ ranks problems by Euro impact and headcount. Small-country mandate pressure is structurally invisible at group level until it becomes an emergency.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

